# What GitHub shows about this repository, and why.
#
# GitHub decides a project's language by counting bytes per file, using Linguist.
# Two things need saying, and one thing must NOT be said.

# bootstrap/boot.c is 4600 lines of C that exist only to compile the compiler once,
# after which the language builds itself. Counted plainly it would make a self-hosting
# language look like a C project, which is the opposite of the claim it makes.
bootstrap/boot.c linguist-vendored

# .wz is mapped to Pascal, and that is a deliberate compromise rather than a mistake.
#
# Linguist does not know Wantzel, and it will not for a long time. Its rule, checked
# against github-linguist/linguist CONTRIBUTING.md on 15-09-2026: an extension expected
# to occur more than once per repository needs at least 2000 files indexed in the last
# year, spread across a reasonable number of distinct user/repo combinations -- so one
# large project of our own does not count towards it. Until then there are two options, and only one of them works:
#
#   *.wz linguist-language=Wantzel   -- FAILS SILENTLY. Linguist looks the name up,
#       finds nothing, and treats every .wz file as undetected: no highlighting, and
#       excluded from the language bar. That is worse than doing nothing, so it must
#       not go in this file however much we would prefer it to be true.
#
#   *.wz linguist-language=Pascal    -- works. Pascal is a language Linguist knows,
#       so the files are counted and highlighted. The grammar is not exact, but it
#       agrees on what matters here: begin/end, :=, strings, numbers. Pascal also
#       highlights { } as a comment, which Wantzel stopped doing in 0.2.0 -- so a brace
#       in a .wz file shows as a comment on GitHub and is a syntax error to the compiler.
#       That is a cosmetic wrongness in the direction that matters least.
#
# The label is wrong and the highlighting is right. Until Linguist knows the language,
# that trade is the better of the two.
*.wz linguist-language=Pascal
